[description]

[b]GitHub Actions[/b] is a CI/CD and general automation system triggered by [i]events[/i] on the repository: [code]push[/code], [code]pull_request[/code], [code]schedule[/code] (cron), [code]workflow_dispatch[/code] (manual), releases, issue comments, and many more. Workflows are YAML files under [code].github/workflows/[/code]. Because they live in Git, every change can go through the same review culture as application code.

[b]Jobs and steps:[/b] a workflow contains one or more [i]jobs[/i] that run in parallel by default. Each job specifies a [i]runner[/i] (GitHub-hosted VM or your self-hosted agent) and an ordered list of [i]steps[/i]—either shell commands or reusable actions from the marketplace. Steps share the job’s workspace, which is a fresh clone of your repo (unless you customize checkout depth for speed).

[b]How this relates to Git:[/b] Actions does not replace local Git hooks. A developer might still use [code]pre-commit[/code] for fast lint on their laptop; Actions proves the same commands on a clean environment every push. When a check fails, the PR stays unmergeable if branch protection says so—the Git objects already exist on the PR branch; policy simply blocks integrating them into [code]main[/code].

[b]Secrets and contexts:[/b] repository and organization secrets are injected as environment variables or inputs to actions without being written into logs—when configured carefully. [b]Environments[/b] add optional manual approval gates, wait timers, and scoped secrets per deployment target (staging vs production).

[b]Caching and artifacts:[/b] dependency caches speed up repeated runs; [i]artifacts[/i] retain build outputs (test reports, binaries) for later jobs or human download. Both are host storage, not Git objects.

[b]Third-party actions supply chain:[/b] pinning actions to full commit SHAs instead of floating branch names reduces the risk of a tag being moved maliciously. Review action source like any dependency.

[b]Self-hosted runners:[/b] useful for proprietary hardware or huge builds, but you inherit patching and trust: a compromised runner can exfiltrate secrets from workflows that target it.

[b]Matrix builds and reuse:[/b] run the same job over a dimension (OS × Node version) to widen coverage; extract common steps into composite actions or reusable workflows to avoid YAML copy-paste drift.

[b]Concurrency groups:[/b] cancel superseded runs when someone pushes again quickly—saves minutes and keeps status checks meaningful on the latest commit only.

[b]Interaction with merge queue / merge group:[/b] some orgs run extra checks on a temporary integration branch GitHub creates when merging stacked PRs. Still Git refs—just orchestrated.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] think “workflow YAML is program code, runners are machines, events are inputs.” Git records what shipped; Actions proves how it was tested before it shipped.
